   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Polynomial Model\n",
    "\n",
    "We will use a polynomial model of degrees of 1, 2, and 3 and calculate the R^2 of each one."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 131,
   "id": "1b284da9",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "degree 1 -> R^2: 0.843\n",
      "degree 2 -> R^2: 0.88\n",
      "degree 3 -> R^2: 0.155\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "from sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\n",
    "from sklearn.preprocessing import PolynomialFeatures\n",
    "for degree in [1, 2, 3]:\n",
    "    poly_model = make_pipeline(PolynomialFeatures(degree), LinearRegression())\n",
    "    scores = cross_val_score(poly_model, x_scaled_train, y_train, cv=4)\n",
    "    print(\"degree\", degree, \"-> R^2:\", round(scores.mean(), 3))\n",
    "###"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "67c31240",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "As we observe now, the polynomial model with degree of 2 gives higher R^2 than the others."
